Primality certificates for the large cofactors
==============================================

Why this exists
---------------
../znam/NOTES ends its list of reasons for trust with "one honest gap": the
k = 8 enumeration decides primality of cofactors with the Miller-Rabin test to
the twelve bases 2..37.  That test is a proof only below

        psi_12 = 318665857834031151167461      (about 3.19e23)

(Sorenson & Webster, "Strong pseudoprimes to twelve prime bases" - quoted from
memory, like everything bibliographic on this box), and some cofactors are
larger.  If one of them were a composite that fools all twelve bases, the
divisor pairs that split it would never have been looked at, and a solution
could be missing from k8.sols.

This folder closes that gap, and does the same for the search in ../odd.
The searches were rerun with every "prime" verdict on an integer >= psi_12
written to a file; then a certificate of primality was produced and checked
for each one.

                                  integers the search     certificates     largest
                                  treated as prime        accepted
    ../znam, k = 8                     201,987              210,608        4.5e26
    ../odd,  k = 12, odd terms          61,880               70,946        9.9e32

(The surplus certificates are for primes that other certificates lean on.)
No integer on either list failed.  So neither result depends on an unproved
primality claim any more.  They still depend on the psi_12 bound, on the
search code, and on the two programs here; see "What this does not show".

What a certificate is
---------------------
One line per integer n:      n  a  F1  P  Q  F2
where F1 and F2 are lists q:e of prime powers ("-" for an empty F2).
verify.py accepts the line only if all of the following hold.

  (1) Each q is prime: either q < psi_12 and q passes the twelve-base test, or
      q has an accepted certificate of its own (q < n, so there is no circle).
  (2) Each q^e in F1 divides n - 1.   a^(n-1) = 1 (mod n),   and for each q in
      F1,   gcd(a^((n-1)/q) - 1, n) = 1.
  (3) If F2 is not empty: each q^e in F2 divides n + 1.  With D = P^2 - 4Q,
      gcd(n, 2QD) = 1.  With U_0 = 0, U_1 = 1, U_(j+1) = P U_j - Q U_(j-1):
      U_(n+1) = 0 (mod n),  and for each q in F2,  gcd(U_((n+1)/q), n) = 1.
  (4) Write F1, F2 for the products of the listed prime powers, and
      L = lcm(F1, F2).  No integer d with 1 < d <= sqrt(n), d = 1 (mod F1) and
      d = +1 or -1 (mod F2) divides n.  (That is at most two residue classes
      mod L; verify.py walks them, and refuses a certificate for which the
      walk would take more than 5 million steps.)

Why that proves n prime
-----------------------
Let p be any prime factor of n.

Lemma A.  If (2) holds for q^e, then p = 1 (mod q^e).
  The order w of a modulo p divides n - 1.  It does not divide (n-1)/q, for
  then p would divide both a^((n-1)/q) - 1 and n.  So q divides w to the full
  power to which it divides n - 1, which is at least e; and w divides p - 1.

Lemma B.  If (3) holds, there is a sign s = +1 or -1, depending only on p,
with p = s (mod q^e) for every q^e in F2.
  Work in the ring A = F_p[x]/(x^2 - Px + Q).  Put alpha = x, beta = P - x; then
  alpha + beta = P, alpha beta = Q, (alpha - beta)^2 = D.  As p does not divide
  2QD, alpha, beta and alpha - beta are units, and in A
        U_j = (alpha^j - beta^j)/(alpha - beta)
  (both sides satisfy the same recurrence and agree for j = 0, 1).  Let
  tau = alpha/beta and let w be its order; then p | U_j exactly when w | j.
    If D is a square mod p, A is F_p x F_p and w divides p - 1.
    If not, A is the field with p^2 elements, its Frobenius map swaps alpha
    and beta, tau^(p+1) = (alpha beta)/(beta alpha) = 1, and w divides p + 1.
  By (3), w divides n + 1 but not (n+1)/q; so q^e | w, and w | p - s.
  (verify.py gets U_j mod n as the coefficient of x in x^j, computed in
  (Z/n)[x]/(x^2 - Px + Q); that is right because x^j = U_j x - Q U_(j-1).)

So p = 1 (mod F1) and p = +-1 (mod F2).  A composite n has a prime factor
p <= sqrt(n), and that p would be one of the d in (4).  There is none.

These are the classical n - 1 and n + 1 tests (Pocklington, Lehmer, Morrison,
Brillhart-Lehmer-Selfridge) in about the crudest combined form there is: no
cube-root refinement, only "few enough candidates are left to try them all".

How prove.py finds certificates
-------------------------------
  - The gcd of n - 1 and of n + 1 with the product of all primes below 10^6
    gives their smooth parts.  An unfactored cofactor that is itself prime is
    used too: for free if it is below psi_12, otherwise after getting a
    certificate of its own ("recursive" below).
  - If lcm(F1, F2) is still so small that (4) would need more than 10^5 trial
    divisions, Brent's rho runs on the composite cofactors of n - 1 and n + 1,
    with a doubling budget, until enough has been split off.
  - If n - 1 alone is enough, F2 is left empty.

    Per proof (helper primes included), for the odd k = 12 list, which ran in
    one go:   70,946 proofs  =  54,319 straight from the smooth parts
                              +  9,066 that first needed a certificate for a
                                       large prime cofactor
                              +  7,561 that needed rho (1.4e9 iterations in all),
              counted by the last tool used.
    About 1,800 s on a shared core, most of it in rho; step (4) took 1.0e8
    trial divisions.  The k = 8 run was interrupted (next paragraph), so its
    counters are split over two logs; the part after the restart was 122,460
    proofs = 104,532 + 7,983 + 9,945, in about 1,400 s.

  One input is worth a mention.  3262626949329269334909599 is 7 mod 8 and
  n + 1 is divisible by 2^5 3 5^2 7, and every integer from 2 to 60 is a
  quadratic residue modulo it.  Condition (3) for q = 2 needs Q to be a
  non-residue, and the first version of prove.py only tried 2 <= Q < 60: it
  stopped with "no Lucas parameters" 87,510 integers into the k = 8 list.
  It now tries negative Q as well (here Q = -1 does it), and base a = 61.

How much to trust it
--------------------
  - verify.py shares no code with prove.py and does not care how a
    certificate was found.  It is about 130 lines, and the two lemmas above
    are all it implements.
  - fuzz_lemmas.py: for every odd composite n < 2500, every base a, every
    (P, Q) in a small box, and every single prime power (and the full list)
    dividing n -+ 1: whenever conditions (2) or (3) hold, the conclusion of
    the lemma is compared with the actual prime factors of n.  For composites
    the conditions held 6,969 times (n - 1) and 1,998 times (n + 1); the
    conclusion never failed.
  - fuzz_verify.py: random and hand-picked composites (Carmichael numbers,
    strong and Lucas pseudoprimes, p(2p-1), p(p+2), p^2) with certificates
    built from the true factorisations of n -+ 1: none accepted, while every
    prime below 3000 gets some certificate accepted.
  - prove.py raises an error rather than write a certificate if a Fermat or
    Lucas condition fails or a trial divisor turns up.  It never did: every
    one of the 263,867 large "probable primes" is prime.  Nobody should be
    surprised - no composite is known to pass even a few of these bases by
    accident at this size.  The point was to stop relying on that.

What this does not show
-----------------------
  - That the lists are the right lists.  ../znam/bigprimes.k8.xz was written
    by a rerun of the k = 8 search with a tap on the two places where it asks
    "is this prime?" (a patched copy of znam.py; ../znam/drive_logged.py does
    the same without patching anything).  That rerun gave the same 122
    solutions and route counts as the first run, and ../odd/odd.py with ODD=0
    met exactly the same 201,987 integers.
    ../odd/odd12.bigprimes.xz is written by ../odd/odd.py itself.
  - Anything about the rest of the search logic.  The NOTES in ../znam and
    ../odd say what was cross-checked there.
  - The psi_12 bound itself.  For what it is worth, the searches were already
    leaning on that bound for every smaller cofactor.

Rerunning
---------
    python3 verify.py k8.cert.xz ../znam/bigprimes.k8.xz           # ~2 min
    python3 verify.py odd12.cert.xz ../odd/odd12.bigprimes.xz      # ~1 min
    python3 fuzz_lemmas.py 2500 ; python3 fuzz_verify.py 1
    python3 prove.py ../znam/bigprimes.k8.xz new.cert              # ~15-30 min; rho is randomised,
                                                                   #   so the lines differ from k8.cert

Second session on this box, November 2026, by Claude Fable 5.2.
